Now I have the full picture. This is an MSTest 4.x integration test class with 8 test methods, marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`. Let me compile the formal audit.

---

## 🔬 testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Audit

**Scope:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` — 8 test methods, MSTest 4.1, .NET 10  
**Calibration:** Integration-test rules applied (class bears `[TestCategory("Integration")]`). Mystery Guest, Eager Test, and Sleepy Test severity are adjusted per integration norms where appropriate.

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

### Findings by Severity

#### ⛔ HIGH — Smell 3: Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)  
**Academic name:** Sleepy Test  
**Integration calibration:** Thread.Sleep remains a smell even in integration tests — it introduces non-deterministic timing and inflates suite duration.

```csharp
// ❌ Current
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead:**
```csharp
// ✅ Fixed
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
{
    await Task.Delay(50, cts.Token);   // fast poll, bounded timeout
}
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Risk if unfixed:** 3-second hard sleep on every run; flaky failures if the system is slower than 3 s; false passes if the event fires *after* 3 s in CI.

---

#### ⛔ HIGH — Smell 1: Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)  
**Academic name:** Conditional Test Logic

```csharp
// ❌ Current — one branch may never execute
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

The `if`/`else` means **only one assertion path ever runs** for a given execution. If the data always yields a basic `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code — a hidden testing gap.

**Fix — split into two focused tests with deterministic setup:**
```csharp
// ✅ Fixed
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");
    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(((PremiumUser)loaded).DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is silently never tested; a future regression in the untested branch won't be caught.

---

#### ⛔ HIGH — Smell 4: Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)  
**Academic name:** Unknown Test / Empty Test

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion; passes even if inserts silently fail
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

The name implies "runs without errors" but if `Insert` silently swallows exceptions or inserts zero rows, this still passes.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**
```csharp
// ✅ Fixed
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage report shows this code as tested, but no behavior is actually verified.

---

#### ⚠️ MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (Extended Catalog)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`, method `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)  
**Academic name:** Assertion Roulette

```csharp
// ❌ Current — 4 assertions, none with failure messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

When one of these fails in CI, the test runner reports the method name and the assertion type, but not *which property* was wrong without reading the source.

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
// ✅ Fixed
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Id should be assigned by the database");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower failure diagnosis, especially in CI logs where you can't click through to source.

---

### ✅ Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Designed Tests)

The remaining **4 tests** are well-structured:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused on one behavior, meaningful assertions |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Clean negative check with `Assert.IsNull` — verifies absence, not just no-crash |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | Count assertion (3) is contextually obvious (3 inserts precede it) — not a Magic Number smell |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await, meaningful count assertion, tests a real concurrency concern |

The **General Fixture** (Smell 9) check also passes cleanly — both `_connection` and `_repository` are used by all 8 tests.

### 🧹 Smells Not Detected (Clean on 15 of 19)

No instances found for: Mystery Guest, Eager Test, Magic Number Test, Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling in Tests, General Fixture, Ignored Test, Duplicate Assert, Lazy Test, Constructor Initialization, Default Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Resource Optimism, or Empty Test.

---

### Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Test | Effort | Why first |
|----------|-------|------|--------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` | ~2 min | Eliminates a false-pass — highest risk-to-effort ratio |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` | ~5 min | Removes a hidden untested branch |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` | ~10 min | Removes 3 s of dead wait + flakiness risk |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` | ~2 min | Quality-of-life for CI failure diagnosis |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests are clean and well-designed. The other 4 each carry exactly one smell. Fixes 1 and 4 are two-minute changes; fixes 2 and 3 need a bit more thought but are straightforward. No systemic design problems — this is a solid integration suite with a few targeted issues.
